## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of integration)
## DBsection(Work)
## KEYWORDS('definite integral', 'work')
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Author(Brian Burns)
## Level(4)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('6.6')
## Problem1('9')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$B = random(2,  5,  2);
$c = random(10, 50, 10);

$H = Formula("x^2/2")->reduce;
Context("Fraction");
$C1 = Compute("($B)**2/2");
$C2 = Compute("$c*2/$B/$B");

Context("Numeric");
#   Problem Statement

# Strings in Tex form (formula)

BEGIN_PGML

Assume that [$c] ft-lb of work is required to stretch a spring [$B] ft beyond its natural length. What is the spring constant?

    [`k =`] [_]{$C2} [`\;\textrm{lb/ft}`]

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
With [`F(x)=k \cdot x,\; \;a=0,\;b=[$B]`]

[```
\begin{aligned}
\textrm{Work Done} & =\int_{a}^{b} F(x) \;dx\\
& = k\int_{0}^{[$B]}  x\;dx \\
& =k\left[[$H]\right]_{0}^{[$B]}=[$C1] k= [$c]
\end{aligned}
```]
Therefore,
[```k=[$C2]```]
The Spring Constant is [`k=[$C2]`] lb/ft.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
